          "text": "I can help \u2014 tell me more so I can give specific steps. Quick questions:\n- What language/framework/environment (Python, JS/Node, Java, C/C++, Go, Rust, mobile, web, Docker, etc.)?\n- What are the symptoms (error messages, wrong output, crash, hang, slow)?\n- Exact error text/stack trace and relevant code (or a minimal reproducible example).\n- How to reproduce it (commands, inputs, steps).\n- What you\u2019ve already tried and results.\n\nWhile you prepare that, here\u2019s a short, practical debugging checklist and common tools/commands you can try now.\n\nGeneral step-by-step debugging checklist\n1. Reproduce reliably\n   - Make the bug happen consistently. If intermittent, capture logs and simplify inputs.\n2. Get the exact failure info\n   - Copy full error message, stack trace, runtime logs, timestamps.\n3. Reduce to a minimal reproducible example\n   - Remove unrelated code until the bug still appears. This often reveals the cause.\n4. Read the stack trace and follow the call path\n   - Look at the topmost frames in your code (not library code) and inspect variables/arguments.\n5. Inspect state with prints/logging or an interactive debugger\n   - Print inputs/outputs, or set breakpoints to examine variables and flow.\n6. Isolate via binary search\n   - Comment out or bypass half the logic to narrow where the fault appears.\n7. Check assumptions and edge cases\n   - Nulls, async timing, off-by-one, wrong units, encoding, permissions, environment differences.\n8. Add unit/integration tests to lock the failure and prevent regressions.\n9. Use specialized tools for memory, concurrency, or performance bugs\n   - Sanitizers, race detectors, profilers, leak detectors.\n10. If all else fails, git bisect to find the commit that introduced the bug.\n\nCommon language-specific tools/commands\n\n- Python\n  - Run tests: pytest -q\n  - Debugger: python -m pdb script.py or use ipdb; in VS Code / PyCharm set breakpoints.\n  - Print traceback: import traceback; traceback.print_exc()\n  - Check types: mypy (optional)\n  - Profiling: cProfile, py-spy\n- JavaScript / Node\n  - Console/logging: console.log\n  - Node inspector: node --inspect-brk script.js and open Chrome DevTools\n  - Browser: DevTools (breakpoints, network, performance)\n  - Linting: eslint\n- C / C++\n  - Compile with debug symbols: -g\n  - Run in gdb: gdb --args ./app\n  - ASan/TSan: compile with -fsanitize=address,thread\n  - Valgrind: valgrind --leak-check=full ./app\n- Go\n  - Race detector: go run -race or go test -race\n  - Debugger: dlv (Delve)\n  - Profiling: pprof\n- Java\n  - Stack traces from exceptions indicate class/method/line.\n  - jstack for thread dumps, VisualVM for profiling.\n- Rust\n  - cargo test, cargo run\n  - Backtraces: RUST_BACKTRACE=1\n  - Miri for undefined behavior, AddressSanitizer support via rustflags\n- Containers / K8s\n  - docker logs <container>, docker exec -it <container> /bin/sh\n  - kubectl logs/pods, describe pod, kubectl exec\n\nTips for specific categories of bugs\n- Crashes / segfaults: enable debug symbols, run under gdb/valgrind/ASan, inspect core, check dangling pointers and buffer bounds.\n- Memory leaks: use Valgrind, heap profilers, or language-specific leak detectors.\n- Concurrency/race: use thread sanitizer / race detector, reproduce with stress tests, add logging of thread ids.\n- Performance regressions: use profilers (perf, pprof, py-spy, Chrome DevTools) to find hotspots.\n- Network/API errors: capture HTTP requests (curl -v, Postman, tcpdump, browser network tab); check timeouts and auth headers.\n- Database issues: enable query logging, check indexes, run EXPLAIN, confirm environment (staging vs prod) credentials.\n\nHow to share useful debug info here\n- Minimal reproducible code snippet (as small as possible)\n- Exact error/stack trace and the line it points to\n- Environment info: OS, runtime version (python --version / node --version / go version), dependencies/versions\n- Steps/commands to reproduce\n\nIf you paste the minimal code and the error/stack trace now, I\u2019ll walk through it and suggest concrete fixes and commands to run."
